# RELEASE — Versioning & Release Workflow
File: openOODA/practices/release.oot

This is the day-to-day workflow for shipping a release in any openOODA repo. The protocol
is specified in `openOODA/rfcs/0006-versioning-and-release-protocol.oot`; the law that
backs it is `RULES.oot §1.24`. This file is the cheat sheet.

## The 4-Step Sequence

Every release is exactly these four steps, in this order, no skipping:

```
1. Bump VERSION
2. Commit
3. Tag
4. Push + Release
```

The runtime check (`openOODA/scripts/release_invariant.oo`) runs at step 3 and refuses to
let you tag if `VERSION` does not match the proposed tag. Drift becomes a failure, not a
footgun.

## Step 1: Bump VERSION

Edit the `VERSION` file at the repo root. Two fields must change together:

```
<repo>=X.Y.Z            # the new product version
release_tag=X.Y.Z      # the assertion of the new release
```

Other fields (`ooda=`, `ooda_max=`, `api_surface=`, etc.) change as needed by the change
itself. The `release_tag=` line is the one that the runtime check verifies against the
git tag.

### When to bump MAJOR vs MINOR vs PATCH

- **MAJOR**: API change that breaks consumers. Renamed or removed public symbols. New
  required fields. Callers must update their imports.
- **MINOR**: backwards-compatible feature. New public symbols, new optional fields, new
  ANCHOR.oo, new `release_tag=` enforcement.
- **PATCH**: backwards-compatible bug fix. ANCHOR.oo content corrections, stale
  `File:` references, VERSION drift recovery, structural enforcement scripts.

## Step 2: Commit

```
git add VERSION
git commit -m "<repo>: vX.Y.Z — <one-line summary of the change>"
```

The commit message starts with the repo name and version. The summary is one line. Full
context goes in the GitHub release notes at step 4, not in the commit message.

## Step 3: Tag (the structural check fires here)

```
git tag vX.Y.Z
```

The tag name MUST equal the `release_tag=` value in `VERSION`, with a `v` prefix. The
runtime check (`openOODA/scripts/release_invariant.oo`) reads the proposed tag name, reads
the `VERSION` file, and exits non-zero if they disagree. No tag is created if the check
fails.

The check is a pure read — it does not modify the tag, the file, or any state. It is
safe to invoke any time:

```
# Manual verification (any time, no side effects)
ooda run openOODA/scripts/release_invariant.oo
```

## Step 4: Push and Release

```
git push origin main vX.Y.Z
gh release create vX.Y.Z \
  --title "vX.Y.Z — <short marketing title>" \
  --notes "<release notes>"
```

The `--title` MUST start with the version (`vX.Y.Z — ...`) so the release list is
self-describing. The `--notes` is a multi-line description: what changed, why, and the
artifact summary (companion commits, deploy steps, etc.).

## Drift Recovery

If `VERSION` and the most recent tag disagree, the repo is in drift. Recovery depends
on which one is ahead:

### Case A: VERSION older than the tag (the common mistake)

The tag was made without bumping VERSION. Revert the tag, bump VERSION, re-tag, re-release.

```
# 1. Delete the local and remote tag
git tag -d vX.Y.Z
git push origin :refs/tags/vX.Y.Z

# 2. Fix VERSION
# (edit <repo>= and release_tag= to the right value)

# 3. Commit, tag, push, release
git add VERSION
git commit -m "<repo>: vX.Y.Z — drift recovery (VERSION < tag)"
git tag vX.Y.Z
git push origin main vX.Y.Z
gh release create vX.Y.Z --title "vX.Y.Z — drift recovery" --notes "..."
```

### Case B: VERSION newer than the tag

The VERSION was bumped but the tag was not made. Tag from the current HEAD at the value
VERSION declares.

```
git tag vX.Y.Z    # the value VERSION declares
git push origin main vX.Y.Z
gh release create vX.Y.Z --title "vX.Y.Z — ..." --notes "..."
```

## Common Mistakes

- **Tagging without bumping VERSION.** Creates drift. Recovery: Case A above.
- **Bumping VERSION without tagging.** The change is in `main` but not released. Recovery:
  Case B above.
- **Release title does not match tag.** The release list is unsorted and unauditable.
  Recovery: delete the release (`gh release delete vX.Y.Z`) and re-create with the right
  title.
- **Multiple tags for the same commit.** The release list shows two versions for one
  change. Recovery: delete the duplicate tag.

## Why this is a practice, not a law

The 4-step sequence is mechanical and inviolable (see `RULES.oot §1.24`). The
*judgment* — when to bump MAJOR vs MINOR, what the release notes say, how to phrase
the title — evolves with the project. The law fixes the invariant; this practice is the
working how-to.

